  "query_preview": "..This morning...The shortwave trough spurring \nactivity in northwest Arizona has propagated well to our \nnortheast, and the last bit of convective activity is quickly \nlosing intensity early this morning under subsidence. Expect \nclearing skies with slightly chillier morning lows than previous \ndays.\n\nToday through Thursday...The large west coast trough continues to \ndeepen and bring stronger southwest flow across northern AZ. This \nwill introduce drier air with PW falling to the 40th-60th percentile \nfor early September. There is still enough moisture for some \nconvective activity, but just isolated showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected each afternoon/evening. The troughing will also bring \nnoticeably breezier daytime winds, with gusts up to 30 mph strongest \nin northwest AZ. Also expect daytime temperatures a few degrees \nbelow normal each day. Overall expect a reprieve from the recently \nactive late monsoon conditions.\n\nFriday through Monday...The forecast uncertainty ramps up \nconsiderably this weekend. The trough looks to gradually back off \nthrough this period. This would open the door for the flow turn more \nsoutherly and moisture to stream back in, first to White Mountains \nand then to most of northern AZ. The most uncertain factor during \nthis period is a potential tropical system moving off the coast of \nthe Baja Peninsula, which could impact the magnitude of moisture. \nThe ensemble consensus is for the moisture to return, with the LREF \nmean PW climbing back above the 90th percentile by Saturday. The \nforecast maintains a 30-60% chance of precipitation each day, higher \nin the White Mountains.",
